-- The design note's own shared-module shape (docs/rsp-microcode-in-pak.md,
-- "The ABI is a struct both sides `use`"): a struct in a `module`, meant to
-- be `use`d by a microcode and (on a real CPU-side driver) the program
-- that DMAs data in and out of it. This file declares nothing else -- no
-- `entry`, no functions -- exactly like the note's own `src/shared/
-- vtxjob.pk64` example.
--
-- Deliberately NOT `use`d by a CPU-side driver in this test project: the
-- vec8x16 fields have no CPU-side representation (vec8x16 is the RSP's
-- vector register, meaningless to the VR4300 -- see rsp_codegen.tcl's own
-- comments), so a driver still addresses this struct's DMEM image as raw
-- bytes at known offsets, the same way every rsp_task_*_driver.pk64 here
-- already does. What THIS test proves is the other half: that
-- `../rsp/transform.pk64`'s `use shared.vtxjob` actually resolves this
-- file's struct via cli.tcl's pak::rsp_resolve_program, instead of
-- rsp_codegen.tcl's existing single-file-only struct registration failing
-- to find it.

module shared.vtxjob

@aligned(16)
struct VtxJob {
    mvp: [4]vec8x16,
    count: i32,
    vertices: [4]vec8x16
}
